rt` stay `int` (`:256,:271` → `:318,:333`).\n- `Read_FileMon`'s `unsigned int i`/`timeout` → `c_uint` (`file_queue.rs:265,267`).\n\n**4. Off-by-one in buffer sizes — no divergence.**\n- `read-alert.c:101-104`: `char str[OS_MAXSTR + 1]; str[OS_MAXSTR] = '\\0'; fgets(str, OS_MAXSTR, fp)` → `read_alert.rs:152-157`: `[0 as c_char; OS_MAXSTR + 1]`, `*str.add(OS_MAXSTR) = 0`, `fgets(str, OS_MAXSTR as c_int, fp)`. Both 1025-byte array / 1024 `fgets` limit. Fuzzed with lines of exactly 1015-1035 bytes and the split-line continuations they produce: identical.\n- `file-queue.h:28` `char mon[4]` + `file-queue.c:125` `strncpy(...,3)` → `file_queue.rs:36` + `:110`. `mon[3]` is left untouched by both (it stays 0 only because `driver` memsets the struct); I dumped all 4 raw bytes of `mon` for all 12 months × 15 flag values — identical.\n- `file-queue.h:29` `char file_name[MAX_FQUEUE+1]` + `file-queue.c:57-60` (`snprintf(..., MAX_FQUEUE, ...)`, plus the explicit `[0]` and `[MAX_FQUEUE]` stores) → `file_queue.rs:37,138-152`. 257-byte array / 256 limit in both. I dumped all 257 bytes — identical.\n- `file-queue.c:25-26` `char buffer[256]; snprintf(buffer, sizeof(buffer), ...)` → `file_queue.rs:60-63` `[0 as c_char; 256]` + `size_of_val(&buffer)` = 256. Fuzzed 10 templates × 16 filename lengths (0-600, straddling 255/256/257) × 6 `err` values: identical truncation.\n\n**5. Control flow / `goto l_error` vs `break 'l_error` — no divergence.** Exact 1:1 correspondence, verified by grep:\n- 6 `goto l_error` (`read-alert.c:114,186,193,219,227,239`) ↔ 6 `break 'l_error` (`read_alert.rs:171,249,256,282,290,302`).\n- 6 `continue` (`:122,133,141,163,167,200`) ↔ 6 `continue` (`:179,193,202,226,230,263`); each Rust `continue` binds to the `while` loop, not the labelled block, so semantics match.\n- 2 early `return al_data` (`:112,306`) ↔ (`:169,368`).\n- The specific path you flagged: when `fgets` returns NULL and `feof(fp) && _r == 2` is **false**, C falls off the end of the `if` into `l_error:`. In Rust the `if feof(fp) != 0 && _r == 2 { return al_data; }` at `:367-369` is the last statement of the `'l_error` block, so falling out of the block lands on `FreeAlertData(al_data); clearerr(fp); ptr::null_mut()` at `:374-377` — identical. Exercised by empty files, header-only files (`_r == 1`), files whose last line is a header, and error-not-EOF streams (`fopen` on a directory → `fgets` fails with `EISDIR`, `feof` false): all identical, including the `clearerr` effect (I compared `feof(fp)` after the call returns).\n\n**6. `FreeAlertData` field set and order — no divergence.** `read-alert.c:66-74` frees exactly `alertid, date, location, comment, group, srcip, dstip, user, filename`, then `free(al_data)` at `:88`. `read_alert.rs:119-133` frees the same nine in the same order, then `free(al_data as *mut c_void)`. To be explicit about your note: there is **no** `char*` field the C leaves unfreed — `srcport`/`dstport` are `int`s, the `al_data->log` cleanup at `read-alert.c:77-85` is commented out *and the `log` member no longer exists in the struct* (`read-alert.h:25-39`), and the `char **p;` local at `read-alert.c:65` is unused. The Rust correctly omits both (comment at `read_alert.rs:117,130`). `al_data = NULL;` at `read-alert.c:89` is a dead store to the parameter; correctly dropped (`read_alert.rs:134`).\n\n**7. Variable resets — no divergence.** `_r = 1` at `:162`→`:225`; `_r = 2` at `:198`→`:261`; `log_size = 0` at `:199`→`:262` (and *only* there — the increment is commented out in both, so `log_size < LOG_LIMIT` is permanently true; confirmed with >100 log lines); `issyscheck = 1` at `:157`→`:220`; `issyscheck = 0` at `:293`→`:358` (inside the `log_size` branch, *after* the `strncmp`, so it also persists across alerts within one call in both). Initialisers `_r = 0, issyscheck = 0, log_size = 0` at `:98-99`→`:147-149`.\n\n**8. Uninitialised vs zeroed locals — a real source difference, but provably unobservable.** C leaves `str[OS_MAXSTR+1]` (`read-alert.c:101`) and `buffer[256]` (`file-queue.c:25`) as stack garbage; Rust zeroes both (`read_alert.rs:152`, `file_queue.rs:60`). Analysis of the only reachable over-read:\n- Within a single `GetAlertData` call the buffer is reused across `fgets` iterations in *both*, so leftovers from the previous line are identical. The window is only the **first** `fgets` of a call.\n- The only expression that can read past the NUL `fgets` wrote is `p = str + 9` (`read-alert.c:118`). That requires a first line that matches `\"** Alert\"` and is ≤ 9 bytes long — i.e. exactly the 8 bytes `\"** Alert\"` with **no** trailing newline, which forces it to be the file's last line.\n- In that state `_r == 0`, so the `_r == 2` pushback at `:110` is not taken; the garbage can at most drive `_r` to 1, and then `fgets` returns NULL, `feof(fp) && _r == 2` is false, and both fall into `l_error` and return NULL.\n- C cannot crash there either, because `str[OS_MAXSTR] = '\\0'` (`:102`) guarantees `strstr`/`strlen` terminate inside the buffer.\n\nEmpirically: `alerts.log` containing exactly `** Alert` (8 bytes, no newline), run 8× with varying stack/env padding, gave `RESULT=NULL` with empty stderr from both, and the C was self-consistent. Same for `merror`: `snprintf` always NUL-terminates, so the zeroed vs garbage tail of `buffer` is never printed.\n\n**9. `Handle_Queue`'s `flags` parameter vs `fileq->flags` — no divergence.** C tests the **parameter** at `file-queue.c:67` (`!(flags & CRALERT_FP_SET)`) and `:84` (`!(flags & CRALERT_READ_ALL)`); `GetFile_Queue` tests **`fileq->flags`** at `:60`. Rust: `file_queue.rs:161` and `:178` test `flags`; `:141` tests `(*fileq).flags`. Call sites: `Init_FileQueue` passes `fileq->flags` (`file-queue.c:135` → `file_queue.rs:250`); `Read_FileMon` passes the literal `0` at **both** `file-queue.c:150` and `:171` (→ `file_queue.rs:272` and `:294`), which is what makes the second `Handle_Queue` always seek to EOF and always reopen, even when the caller asked for `CRALERT_FP_SET`/`CRALERT_READ_ALL`. `GetAlertData` is passed `fileq->flags` (`:160,:178` → `:282,:301`). I swept all 15 flag values `{0,1,2,4,5,6,8,0x10,0x11,0x14,0x15,0x20,-1,INT_MAX,INT_MIN}` × 12 months through `Init_FileQueue`+`Read_FileMon` and dumped the resulting struct: identical, including the `CRALERT_FP_SET`-with-NULL-`fp` (`return 0`, not `-1`) and `CRALERT_FP_SET`-with-unseekable/unstattable-`fp` (`return -1` + `merror`) paths.\n\n**10. `#[repr(C)]` mirrors vs the C structs — no divergence.** `alert_data` (`read_alert.rs:41-56`) matches `read-alert.h:25-39` field-for-field. `file_queue` (`file_queue.rs:30-40`) matches `file-queue.h:22-33` with `time_t → c_long`, `FILE* → *mut FILE`, `struct stat → cbind::stat`. `cbind.rs:37-53` is the correct glibc x86-64 `struct stat` (144 bytes, `st_mtim` at offset 88, `__pad0`, `__glibc_reserved[3]`), and `st_mtime` is correctly rendered as `st_mtim.tv_sec` (`file_queue.rs:211`). `cbind.rs:58-70` is the correct 56-byte glibc `struct tm` (with `tm_gmtoff`/`tm_zone`). Verified empirically: my harness declares the structs per the *C* headers and reads every field written by the Rust library — all agreed, including `last_change == f_status.st_mtime` and the full 257-byte `file_name`.\n\nAlso checked and matching: `shared.rs` (`OS_MAXSTR=1024`, the `os_free`/`os_clearnl` macros, and the three `os_*` allocators including their no-newline `fprintf` messages and `exit(1)`); `driver.rs` (`tm_year = year` with the `+1900` applied only inside `Init_FileQueue`/`Read_FileMon`); every string literal in the project (extracted and compared as multisets — an exact 1:1 match, including `\"%s\"`, `\"%s\\n\"`, `\"r\"`, both `merror` templates and all 12 month names in order); and `nm -D --defined-only` symbol parity between the two `.so`s.\n\n---\n\n### Testing performed (all read-only, in `$TMPDIR`)\n\n- 4,000 randomly generated `alerts.log` corpora (multi-alert, malformed headers, missing `:`/space/`-`, mail-flag rejection, all `Rule:`/`Src IP:`/`Src Port:`/`Dst *`/`User:`/`Integrity …` variants, empty filenames, unterminated comments, embedded NUL bytes, high-bit bytes, lines of 1015-1035 bytes, files with/without a trailing newline) × 1-4 sequential `GetAlertData` calls on the same `FILE*` (exercising the `fseek` pushback), comparing every struct field, `ftell`, `feof`, and stderr. **0 divergences, 0 C-nondeterminism.**\n- 3,000-case earlier corpus, same result.\n- 960 `merror` invocations (10 templates × 16 lengths × 6 `err` values). 1 differing line, from the deliberately-malformed template `\"%d %s\"` which prints the `file_name` *pointer* via `%d` — address-dependent, and the C is nondeterministic against itself there.\n- 180 `Init_FileQueue` + `Read_FileMon` sweeps (12 months × 15 flags), plus pipe/`fmemopen`/`CRALERT_FP_SET` variants.\n- `driver()` end-to-end: normal, missing `alerts.log` (both 5 s `file_sleep`s), unreadable, directory-as-`alerts.log` (real `FSEEK_ERROR` + errno 22), FIFO (real `FSEEK_ERROR` + errno 29 `Illegal seek` → `Init_FileQueue == -1` → `\"File queue initialization failed\"`), `/dev/null` symlink.\n\n### Three residual differences that are C-side UB and cannot be matched by construction\n\n1. **`s_month[tm_mon]` out of range** — `file-queue.c:125,166` ↔ `file_queue.rs:108-111`. Input: `driver(1, 12, 100, 0, 4)` (or any `month` outside `0..=11`). Both perform the same unchecked read past the 12-entry table, but the *bytes* following the table differ per `.so`, so the resulting `mon` value (or the fault) differs. Already documented as `ERRORS.md` Note B; the Rust deliberately does *not* bounds-check, which is correct.\n2. **`merror` templates with conversions that don't match the three supplied arguments** (e.g. `\"%d %s\"`, `\"%s%s\"`) read arguments as the wrong type / past the varargs list. Address- and garbage-dependent in both; the C is not self-consistent.\n3. **`al_data->filename[strlen(...) - 1]` with an empty filename** (`read-alert.c:290` ↔ `read_alert.rs:355`, input line `Integrity checksum changed for: '` in a `syscheck` alert). Both write one byte before the heap block; `wrapping_sub` reproduces the C faithfully. Observed identical on this glibc, but it is heap corruption in both and not guaranteed to stay identical.","usage":{"total_tokens":135147,"tool_uses":39,"duration_ms":1124039},"uuid":"8981881e-92a1-4b6f-b80f-3634bdaa8fcd","session_id":"28b5e5a5-2f5e-4b09-bc69-ff69bc592e87"}
