{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_npgvmq5q2eo6xd5sk2hh33ntewpes232urmtial3c4bzah4c66fq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Result\n\n**76 differential tests, 0 failures**, run twice for stability. Every test loads *both* `.so`s via `libloading` and calls only exported C symbols.\n\n### Phase A artifacts (`translation/`)\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — 9/9 symbols, `nm -D` diff empty both ways; 0 undefined non-libc/non-runtime symbols. Struct layouts confirmed against a C `offsetof` probe (`file_queue` 440 B, `alert_data` 96 B).\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 39 rows, all checked.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 49 rows, all checked.\n\n### One real translation defect found and fixed\n`src/file_queue.rs::copy_month` had `if tm_mon >= 0 && tm_mon < 12 { strncpy(...) }` — a bounds check the C does not have. Since `driver(day, month, …)` passes `month` straight into `tm_mon`, `driver(1, 12, …)` made the C fault (SIGSEGV) while the Rust returned normally. Replaced with a raw `[*const c_char; 12]` table indexed via `.offset(tm_mon as isize)`, reproducing the C's unchecked read verbatim rather than \"fixing\" it. `err_33c` proves the Rust still faults on out-of-range months, which a bounds-checked version never could.\n\n### Two test-harness bugs fixed (not translation bugs)\n- `merror` with a template demanding more conversions than the 3 arguments supplied reads past the varargs list — my invented `\"%s%s%s%s\"` template was UB, not a testable behaviour.\n- fd 2 is process-global, so stderr-asserting tests were catching concurrent tests' `perror` output. Rows 9–12/35 now live in `tests/phase_c_stderr.rs` (cargo runs test binaries sequentially) plus a mutex.\n\n### Residual differences — all C-side UB, documented as Notes B/C\n1. `s_month[tm_mon]` out of range: both now do the same wild read, but the bytes adjacent to a 12-pointer table differ per `.so`.\n2. `filename[strlen(...) - 1]` with an empty integrity filename: both corrupt the heap chunk header identically (`wrapping_sub`), compared via `waitpid` status in a fork.\n3. C's `char str[OS_MAXSTR+1]` is stack garbage vs Rust's zeroed. Only reachable read past the `fgets` NUL is `p = str + 9` for a bare `\"** Alert\"` at EOF; 1000+ fuzz cases agree.\n\nAn independent line-by-line audit (offsets, `strncmp` argument order incl. the one inverted call site at `read-alert.c:287`, `goto l_error` ↔ `break 'l_error`, `FreeAlertData` field set, `flags` vs `fileq->flags`, `repr(C)` layouts) found no further divergence, and a scripted comparison of all 27 numeric and 21 string `#define`s reported 0 mismatches.\n\nNo `[features]` table exists, so there is one build configuration; the suite was run under both `cargo test` and `--no-default-features`. Neither CMake nor Cargo builds a binary, so there is no stdout to compare. Nothing in `c_src/` was modified.\n\nNote: `cargo` needs `--offline` here (the crates.io proxy is blocked); `libloading 0.8.9` was already in the local registry cache. `run_difftests.sh` builds both `.so`s, checks symbol parity, and runs the suite."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5351,"cache_read_input_tokens":217016,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":5351,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28b5e5a5-2f5e-4b09-bc69-ff69bc592e87","uuid":"0558e239-b959-4237-a484-e092b3e70778","timestamp":"2026-09-05T08:05:08.507Z"}
